import '../../../../../styles/relational-design.css';
const setup = `CREATE TABLE core_products (
  product_id integer PRIMARY KEY,
  name text NOT NULL,
  available integer NOT NULL CHECK (available >= 0)
) WITH (fillfactor = 80);
INSERT INTO core_products
SELECT n, 'Product ' || n, 3
FROM generate_series(1, 10000) AS n;
ANALYZE core_products;`;
const read = `SELECT product_id, name, available
FROM core_products WHERE product_id = 7;`;
const plan = `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)
SELECT product_id, name, available
FROM core_products WHERE product_id = 7;`;
const versions = `SELECT product_id, available, ctid, xmin
FROM core_products WHERE product_id = 7;`;
const reserve = `UPDATE core_products
SET available = available - 2
WHERE product_id = 7 AND available >= 2
RETURNING product_id, available;`;
---
<Base title="Inside PostgreSQL" description="Trace a product lookup and competing reservations through PostgreSQL's pages, buffers, row versions, locks, durable WAL and cleanup.">
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
  <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p><h1>Inside PostgreSQL</h1><TitleLine type="Deep dive" version="PostgreSQL 18" /></header>
<ArticleBody>
  <p>A table looks like a collection of current records. Inside PostgreSQL, it is a collection of pages that can contain several versions of a record, with indexes that help find them and transaction information that decides which version a query may use. A page can be present in memory without being stored on disk. A changed value can be stored on disk without belonging to a committed transaction. Understanding those distinctions explains behaviour that otherwise looks contradictory.</p>
  <p>We will follow one product with three units of stock. An application reads it, reserves two units and commits. Meanwhile, an older report continues reading the earlier value, and another buyer tries to reserve the same stock. The resulting path connects the SQL the application sends to the stored bytes, the concurrency rules and the promise made by a successful commit.</p>
  <p>The worked observations below come from PostgreSQL 18.6 with ordinary logged tables. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=pg-read-write">browser lesson</a> follows a smaller read and update in PGlite. It can expose plans and row locations, but the multi-session schedule and server durability discussion require a PostgreSQL server.</p>

  <section id="connection"><h2>Who receives the request?</h2>
   <p>An application establishes a database connection. In the conventional PostgreSQL server, a <strong>backend process</strong> serves that connection: it receives commands, executes them and returns rows or errors. An application pool may lend the connection to a request handler. An open transaction still belongs to that database session, so the handler must keep using the same connection until it commits or rolls back. Sending the next statement through an arbitrary connection would address a different session.</p>
   <p>Backends share access to tables, a pool of memory holding database pages, and structures that coordinate transactions and locks. They also keep private execution state. For example, a sort can need working memory belonging to an operation in one query. Increasing the number of active connections therefore adds possible concurrent work and private memory demand; it does not create a fresh private copy of the database for each connection. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/tutorial-arch.html">server architecture</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-resource.html">memory settings</a> describe these different kinds of state.</p>
   <p>Other processes work alongside the backends. A checkpointer coordinates checkpoint writes, a background writer helps make buffers reusable, a WAL writer writes log data, and autovacuum workers maintain tables. These are overlapping activities. A request does not pass through each background process like a series of application services.</p>
   <p>Our table has 10,000 products. Product 7 has the name “Product 7” and three available units. The primary key creates a B-tree index. We leave some room on heap pages with <code>fillfactor = 80</code> so that later row versions have a chance to fit on the same page; this is an experiment setting, not a universal tuning recommendation.</p>
   <details><summary>Set up the server experiment</summary><p>Run this once in a disposable database, outside an explicit transaction. The concurrent experiments use separate connections to that same database. Finish their transactions before resetting the table. To repeat from scratch, drop <code>core_products</code> and run the setup again.</p><Sql code={setup} label="Create and commit the starting state" /></details>
   <Sql code={read} label="The application asks for product 7" />
   <p>The answer will be one product. The number 7 is its logical identity; it says nothing about which physical page currently holds it. PostgreSQL must turn that identity into a way to locate and validate a row version.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="read"><h2>How an index leads to a record</h2>
   <p>The server parses the SQL, resolves names and types against its catalogues, and rewrites the query where necessary, for example to expand a view. The planner considers available ways to obtain the answer and estimates their work. The executor then runs the selected plan. An equality condition on a unique indexed key is a promising route to one row, but the SQL text itself does not command a particular physical lookup. This separation is the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/query-path.html">query path</a>.</p>
   <p>A B-tree stores ordered keys. Its upper pages contain boundaries that direct a search into a smaller part of the tree. To find key 7, a search follows the appropriate child at each level until it reaches a leaf page containing the relevant key range. It does not test every leaf entry from the beginning. A matching leaf entry provides a reference into the table’s main storage, called the <strong>heap</strong>.</p>
   <p>The heap is divided into pages, normally 8 KiB in a standard PostgreSQL build. Inside a page, an array of item identifiers points to stored row versions. A tuple location such as <code>(0,7)</code> means page zero, item seven. The item identifier holds the position of the tuple bytes within that page. This extra level of indirection allows the bytes to move during page compaction while the item’s position remains stable. The index can keep referring to that item. PostgreSQL documents these fields in its <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/storage-page-layout.html">page layout</a>.</p>
   <ReadPath />
   <p>In our run, product 7 initially occupied <code>(0,7)</code>. That is an observation from this freshly built table, not an address applications should depend on. The row header contains transaction-related metadata as well as the user values that follow it. Reaching these bytes gives the executor a <em>candidate</em> version. It still has to decide whether that version belongs in the query’s view of the database.</p>
   <Sql code={plan} label="Inspect the route and execute the lookup" />
   <p>The observed plan was an <code>Index Scan using core_products_pkey</code>, with <code>Index Cond: (product_id = 7)</code> and one actual output row. <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> executes the statement, so these are measurements from a real lookup. A query requesting most products can reasonably choose a sequential scan instead: avoiding an index descent is useful when the work already requires most heap pages.</p>
   <p>Our primary-key index supplies the key and a row reference. It cannot supply the requested name or stock value, so the executor visits the heap to obtain them. An index containing all the selected values can sometimes avoid that visit, but having the values is only half the requirement. PostgreSQL also needs a way to establish visibility without reading the row header. We will return to that after following an update.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="buffers"><h2>Where the pages come from</h2>
   <p>The backend accesses ordinary index and heap pages through <strong>shared buffers</strong>. When a needed page is already there, the backend can use the memory copy. Otherwise PostgreSQL obtains the page through its storage layer and brings it into a buffer. That request may be satisfied by the operating system’s cache. A PostgreSQL buffer read therefore does not, by itself, prove that a physical device performed a read.</p>
   <p>The plan in our run reported three shared-buffer hits and no shared-buffer reads. We had just created and analysed the table, so a warm lookup was unsurprising. The counters describe buffer accesses during execution; they should not be read as three distinct pages fetched from disk. This distinction matters when comparing a first run with later runs or trying to explain why the same plan has different latency.</p>
   <p>Backends coordinate access to these shared pages while inspecting or changing them. Those brief memory-level coordination needs differ from the transaction-level wait we will see between two buyers. Finding a row in a cached page does not remove the need to respect an uncommitted writer. Conversely, an ordinary reader does not reserve the whole heap page for the lifetime of its transaction.</p>
   <p>“One row” can also involve storage beyond its main tuple. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/storage-toast.html">TOAST mechanism</a> can compress large values or move them into separate storage. A heap tuple can then contain an external reference. Requesting a large document can require work that a query for its small identifier avoids, even when both queries return one row.</p>
   <p>For this initial read, the setup transaction has committed and no writer has changed product 7. The executor accepts its version, extracts the three requested fields and returns them. The application receives the value three. That response establishes what this read saw; it does not reserve those units against another request.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="versions"><h2>Why readers can see different values</h2>
   <p>Suppose a report begins before the reservation and needs a stable view while it performs several queries. Overwriting three with one in the only copy of the row would destroy the value that this report still needs. PostgreSQL instead creates a new row version for the update and preserves the earlier version while a relevant reader can need it. Both versions represent product 7, but they have different physical locations and different visibility histories.</p>
   <Sql code={versions} label="Inspect the visible version before and after an update" />
   <p><code>ctid</code> exposes a version’s physical location and <code>xmin</code> identifies its inserting transaction. In our experiment, the visible location changed from <code>(0,7)</code> to <code>(0,126)</code> when the reservation committed. Applications should continue identifying the product by its primary key: these <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-system-columns.html">system columns</a> describe versions and physical storage, not a permanent business identity.</p>
   <p>Call the committed setup transaction S and the reservation transaction W. The earlier tuple records that S created it and W replaced it. The replacement records W as its creator. To decide which version to return, a reader needs the outcomes of those transactions <em>and</em> its snapshot: the transaction history included in this read. Knowing that W has committed now is insufficient if the reader must preserve a view established before that commit.</p>
   <p>For this simple case, ask two questions of each version. Is its creation visible in my snapshot? If so, is a later replacement or deletion also visible? The old version remains the answer when S is visible but W is excluded. The new version becomes the answer when W is visible. The full implementation also handles a transaction’s own writes, row-lock metadata and other cases; comparing transaction numbers alone is not a visibility algorithm.</p>
   <VersionChoice />
   <p>The writer’s reservation command is the following conditional update. It returns the new stock only if at least two units remain; keep its transaction open at the indicated point in the schedule.</p>
   <Sql code={reserve} label="The update sent by connection W in the schedule" />
   <p>Here is a schedule using three connections. R is a report at Repeatable Read. W is the writer. N performs ordinary Read Committed reads, each as a new statement. Run the rows in order, waiting for each stated response.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Observed snapshot schedule"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Connection and action</th><th>Observed value and reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>R: <code>BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ</code>, then read product 7</td><td>3. This first read establishes the report’s snapshot.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>W: <code>BEGIN</code>, then reserve two units; leave the transaction open</td><td>The update returns 1 to its own transaction.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>N: read product 7</td><td>3. W has not committed.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>W: <code>COMMIT</code>; then R reads product 7 again</td><td>R still sees 3. Its earlier snapshot excludes W.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>N: perform another read</td><td>1. This statement’s new snapshot includes W.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>R and N use the same database pages, which can contain both versions. PostgreSQL has not copied the entire table for R. It chooses the appropriate version as each reader visits the record. The ordinary read in step 3 can therefore finish while W remains uncommitted. Preserving the earlier version supplies the answer without waiting for W to resolve.</p>
   <p>Change R to Read Committed and predict step 4 again. Its later statement gets a fresh snapshot and can see one after W commits. With Repeatable Read, <code>BEGIN</code> alone does not fix the snapshot: the first non-transaction-control statement establishes it. These details follow PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/transaction-iso.html">isolation rules</a>. End R with <code>COMMIT</code> before moving on. Keeping it open would intentionally retain the older view and affect cleanup.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="writers"><h2>What a competing writer waits for</h2>
   <p>Reset stock to three with a committed <code>UPDATE core_products SET available = 3 WHERE product_id = 7</code>. Two buyers now each request two units. If both application handlers read three and later write their independently calculated answer, the earlier read has done nothing to protect the decision. We put the guard and subtraction in the database operation itself.</p>
   <Sql code={reserve} label="Reserve only if the row being updated has enough stock" />
   <p>The handler sends <code>BEGIN</code>, executes this update and checks its result before continuing. One returned row means this transaction reserved the stock. Zero rows means it did not. A real checkout records the purchase within the same intended transaction boundary and commits only when its required work has succeeded; on failure it rolls back. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/checkout/">checkout walkthrough</a> develops that application flow. Our bounded experiment commits only the stock change.</p>
   <p>Buyer A starts the update and leaves its transaction open. It has produced a replacement with stock one, but that change is not yet committed. Buyer B runs the same update at Read Committed. B cannot treat the old three as an independently writable current value while A’s outcome is unresolved. It waits. In our server run, <code>pg_stat_activity</code> reported a <code>Lock</code> wait on <code>transactionid</code>, and <code>pg_blocking_pids</code> identified A’s backend.</p>
   <p>After A commits, B follows the updated row and re-evaluates its condition. One is less than two, so B returns zero rows. The final stock remains one. The protection comes from coordinating the write and testing the condition against the relevant version after the wait. It does not come from the application having previously received the value three.</p>
   <p>For a useful variation, reset to three and make A roll back instead. B can then reserve from the original three and return one. At stronger isolation levels, a conflicting update can instead require a transaction retry; the application must follow the isolation level’s error contract. In either case, an ordinary read and a competing write have different requirements: the read can use an earlier visible version, while the write must resolve who gets to change the logical row.</p>
   <p>The replacement also affects indexes. Our only indexed column is the unchanged product ID. If the new version fits on the same heap page, a <strong>HOT</strong> update can link versions there without adding ordinary new index entries. The original index reference can lead into that chain. Changing a column referenced by a non-summarising index, or lacking space on the page, prevents this particular shortcut. Including stock in a B-tree would therefore change the maintenance cost of every reservation. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/storage-hot.html">HOT documentation</a> explains these conditions; a changed <code>ctid</code> alone does not prove that HOT occurred.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="commit"><h2>What commit makes durable</h2>
   <p>The update has changed memory copies of pages. Such buffers are called <strong>dirty</strong>: their contents differ from the stored page. PostgreSQL has also generated write-ahead log records, or <strong>WAL</strong>, describing logged changes. A dirty page and a generated log record are intermediate states. Neither alone says that the transaction has committed or survived a machine failure.</p>
   <p>Two ordering requirements connect this work to durability. Before a changed data page is written to persistent storage, the WAL needed to recover that change must be durable. Before acknowledging an ordinary durable local commit, PostgreSQL must make the transaction’s required WAL, including its commit record, durable. It does not need to force every changed heap and index page to storage at that moment. These promises assume logged tables, <code>fsync=on</code>, <code>synchronous_commit=on</code>, and storage that honours flush requests; we are considering local durability without a configured synchronous standby.</p>
   <DurabilityState />
   <p>Imagine the commit response has arrived while the stored heap page still contains stock three. A crash loses the changed memory page. On restart, the durable WAL supplies the changes needed to reconstruct stock one and the transaction’s committed outcome. Writing a sequential log and grouping flushes can make this cheaper than forcing many scattered table and index pages for each transaction. This is the purpose of <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-intro.html">write-ahead logging</a>.</p>
   <p>The reverse ordering is possible too: a changed data page can reach storage before its transaction commits, provided the required WAL has gone first. Its physical presence does not make the new row version visible. Recovery reconstructs physical changes and transaction state; subsequent visibility checks still distinguish committed versions from work that never committed. “The bytes are on disk” and “the transaction succeeded” answer different questions.</p>
   <p>A checkpoint coordinates required dirty-page writes and records a recovery starting point. Restart can begin from the appropriate checkpoint’s redo position instead of replaying the database’s entire history. It does not commit active transactions, and concurrent work can dirty pages again while checkpoint work proceeds. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/recovery/">recovery chapter</a> follows the page and WAL positions that let restart determine which work needs replay.</p>
   <p>Changing the acknowledgement policy changes the promise. Asynchronous commit may return before the commit WAL is durable, exposing recently acknowledged transactions to loss after a crash. Synchronous replication can add requirements involving a standby. Those are deliberate choices about when success may be reported, not evidence that changed table pages must always be forced at commit. A browser <code>COMMIT</code> response cannot test a server’s physical storage guarantees.</p>
   <p>There is one more boundary outside the storage system. A commit can become durable just before the connection disappears, leaving the caller unsure whether it succeeded. The database cannot make receipt of its reply atomic with the caller’s next action. A checkout needs a stable operation identity and a way to reconcile that uncertain outcome before retrying a purchase.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="cleanup"><h2>Vacuum and maintenance after a commit</h2>
   <p>The application now sees stock one, but the earlier version can still occupy space. While R retains the older snapshot, that version has a purpose: it is the report’s answer. Once no relevant snapshot or other retention requirement needs it, page pruning and vacuum can reclaim its space. The timing of logical replacement and physical reclamation are therefore different.</p>
   <p>Vacuum also maintains a <strong>visibility map</strong>, with information about heap pages. When a page is known to contain tuples visible to all transactions, an index-only scan can use that fact instead of visiting each heap tuple to check its header. An index must still contain the required values. An update clears the relevant all-visible information, so a plan called “Index Only Scan” can perform heap fetches until the page becomes eligible again. This explains why adding included columns does not by itself guarantee heap-free reads. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-index-only-scans.html">index-only scan mechanism</a> ties the two requirements together.</p>
   <p>A long-lived snapshot can thus affect later work even while its connection sends no new queries. Retained versions take space, and pages can remain ineligible for the visibility shortcut. More storage does not end the snapshot; a faster vacuum cannot discard a version still required by a reader. First establish whether cleanup is eligible, then investigate whether maintenance has enough capacity to keep up.</p>
   <p>Vacuum also freezes sufficiently old transaction information to protect transaction-ID reuse. <code>ANALYZE</code> does a different job: it gathers statistics used to estimate plans. Autovacuum can schedule both. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/routine-vacuuming.html">maintenance documentation</a> connects these recurring tasks to space reuse, visibility and safe operation. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/row-versions/">row-version chapter</a> examines them through observable versions and counters.</p>
   <p>Our small reservation has now crossed a complete path. A backend chose an index route, obtained pages through shared buffers, selected a visible version, coordinated a replacement with competing writers, made the required WAL durable and left old-version cleanup for later. Each stage explains a different symptom: an accurate index can still lead to a lock wait; a warm query can still do substantial CPU work; a committed change can await a table-page write; and a finished request can leave versions that another session still needs.</p>
   <p>The rest of the series takes each stage of this path further. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/query-execution/">Query execution</a> and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/indexes/">indexes</a> follow the plan choice and the ordered entries behind an index route, <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/row-versions/">row versions</a> examines the visibility and cleanup described here, and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/recovery/">recovery</a> traces what survives a crash. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/replication/">Replication</a> asks what changes when the same history reaches another server, separating receipt, replay and authority to accept writes, and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/sharding/">sharding</a> asks which data and operations belong together when several writers own different subsets. Native partitioning divides one server’s table; distributing ownership across servers needs extra coordination or a system such as Citus. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/json/">JSON</a>, <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/full-text-search/">full-text search</a>, <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/vector-search/">vector search</a> and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/extensions/">types and extensions</a> chapters each start from their own example, so a reader interested in search can begin there.</p>
  </section>
 </ArticleBody>
